{"clips": [
  {
    "id": "C1",
    "weakest_point": "The first 3 seconds are slow, context-free tea banter; a scroller will leave before any scheming or stakes appear. The ending is also a calm statement of intent, not a cliffhanger.",
    "hook": {
      "score": 2,
      "reason": "Above the 3-s line is only Catherine offering sugar and a veiled jab about Rupert's 'sudden climb to power.' It is calm, context-free tea talk; no action, shock, or clear stake lands in the first 3 seconds."
    },
    "cliffhanger": {
      "score": 2,
      "reason": "It ends on Rupert's calm declaration, 'Not through the winter. I've ensured her quiet comfort.' The threat is stated as fact and the scene resolves into silence; there is no unanswered ultimatum or danger bursting in."
    },
    "questions": [
      {
        "q": "Who is Alexander and why does his death matter?",
        "type": "confusion"
      },
      {
        "q": "Will they get away with killing the Dowager?",
        "type": "intrigue"
      }
    ],
    "standalone": {
      "score": 3,
      "reason": "You can tell they are aristocratic plotters, but the relationships—cousin Alexander, the Dowager, the estate—are opaque. You do not know who the wronged party is or what the protagonist stands to lose."
    },
    "emotion": {
      "score": 2,
      "reason": "Distant disdain for their cruelty, but because the victim is an offscreen 'old woman' discussed over tea, there is no immediate injustice to rage against."
    },
    "want_next": {
      "score": 2,
      "reason": "Mild curiosity about their comeuppance, but the lack of an onscreen hero or urgent peril gives little reason to tap for the next episode."
    },
    "ad_policy": {
      "level": "none",
      "category": "none",
      "reason": "Verbal scheming and implied poisoning, but no on-screen violence, blood, or sexual content."
    },
    "one_line_verdict": "Slow-burn villainy that opens with tea talk and closes with a calm threat; too subdued for a stopping scroll."
  },
  {
    "id": "C2",
    "weakest_point": "The cut to a second tea-room moment feels repetitive, and the final beat is another resolved monologue ('This house will only bow to me') rather than a cliffhanger. The opening is punchier than C1 but still just dialogue.",
    "hook": {
      "score": 3,
      "reason": "The first 3 s deliver a cruel, arrogant line—\"'Practically' is a word for beggars. I do not marry a practical Duke.\"—which establishes villainy instantly, but it is still only dialogue with no action or shock."
    },
    "cliffhanger": {
      "score": 2,
      "reason": "It ends on Catherine's regal declaration, 'This house will only bow to me,' as she walks to the window. The scene closes on a resolved character beat rather than an unresolved peak."
    },
    "questions": [
      {
        "q": "Who is the hero these villains are opposing?",
        "type": "confusion"
      },
      {
        "q": "Will their plot against the old Duchess succeed?",
        "type": "intrigue"
      }
    ],
    "standalone": {
      "score": 3,
      "reason": "Clearer than C1 that these are arrogant antagonists, especially with 'Let the peasants rot,' but we still have no hero to root for and no clear wronged victim on screen."
    },
    "emotion": {
      "score": 3,
      "reason": "The 'peasants rot' line and general supremacy provoke dislike, but without an onscreen victim there is no gut punch, only secondhand contempt."
    },
    "want_next": {
      "score": 3,
      "reason": "We want to see them fall, but the ad offers no urgent peril and no protagonist, so the pull to the next episode is moderate."
    },
    "ad_policy": {
      "level": "none",
      "category": "none",
      "reason": "Aristocratic scheming and verbal cruelty; no physical violence or prohibited content."
    },
    "one_line_verdict": "A tighter cut of villainous banter, but still all talk and no urgent peril."
  },
  {
    "id": "C3",
    "weakest_point": "The opening line 'I do not yield to a cold draft' is slightly opaque without context, and the final beat resolves into decisive action (snatching the ledger) rather than cutting at the peak of Prudence's plea.",
    "hook": {
      "score": 3,
      "reason": "A fierce close-up and the defiant line 'I do not yield to a cold draft' stop the scroll better than tea talk, but the line is abstract and the stakes do not fully land until just after the 3-s mark."
    },
    "cliffhanger": {
      "score": 3,
      "reason": "The ad ends as Juliana snatches her ledger to help a dying child, leaving the question of whether she can save him with nothing but coltsfoot, but the peak of Prudence's kneeling plea has already passed."
    },
    "questions": [
      {
        "q": "What is the 'cold draft' Juliana refuses to yield to?",
        "type": "confusion"
      },
      {
        "q": "Can Juliana save Toby with only coltsfoot and no clinic?",
        "type": "intrigue"
      },
      {
        "q": "Why does Seamus say they have reduced her to a peasant?",
        "type": "intrigue"
      }
    ],
    "standalone": {
      "score": 5,
      "reason": "Fully clear with zero context: a fallen noblewoman turned healer, a dying child turned away by the rich, and a life hanging on her next choice. Prudence's plea—\"He is only seven! If you do not come, he will die! You are his only hope, Lady Juliana!\"—makes the stakes unmistakable, which is exactly what the best microdrama ads do."
    },
    "emotion": {
      "score": 4,
      "reason": "The injustice of a doctor laughing at a dying peasant boy and Juliana's defiant dignity create a strong, empathetic pull; the kneeling plea is genuinely moving and the 'reduced me to myself' line lands with quiet power."
    },
    "want_next": {
      "score": 4,
      "reason": "A life-or-death medical crisis paired with a proud heroine who has nothing left gives a powerful reason to tap and see if she pulls off the impossible."
    },
    "ad_policy": {
      "level": "none",
      "category": "none",
      "reason": "A child is ill and pleading, but there is no graphic gore, violence, or harm shown on screen."
    },
    "one_line_verdict": "A clear, emotionally charged rescue setup with a defiant heroine and a dying child; the strongest standalone story of the three."
  }
],
  "best": "C3",
  "best_why": "C3 is the only clip with a clear hero, an onscreen victim, and urgent life-or-death stakes; C1 and C2 are calm villainous banter that violates the core rule that scheming talked about calmly is not drama."
}
